Category: Golden Rules
Purpose:
  The 10 foundational knowledge principles for the .sdd system.
  These principles govern how knowledge is created, maintained,
  and consumed. They serve as the constitution for AI learning behavior.

Owns: ./

Knowledge Principles:

  1. Prefer reuse over creation.
     Before creating any new knowledge element, search for existing
     concepts, references, skills, or patterns that already solve
     the problem. Creation is the last resort.

  2. Prefer linking over copying.
     When existing knowledge applies, reference it. Never duplicate
     knowledge across files. Single source of truth, many pointers.

  3. Prefer specialization over duplication.
     When knowledge is similar but not identical, create a
     specialization (typed edge) rather than a near-duplicate.

  4. Prefer evidence over assumption.
     Every knowledge claim requires evidence: code, tests, decisions,
     production observations, or human validation. Unfounded assertions
     are candidates at best.

  5. Prefer canonical concepts over synonyms.
     When multiple terms describe the same concept, canonicalize to
     the preferred term. Store alternatives as aliases, not new concepts.

  6. Never silently learn.
     When AI observes something new, it MUST declare it as a candidate.
     Undeclared learning is knowledge corruption. All learning is
     visible and traceable.

  7. Never silently overwrite.
     When existing knowledge changes, the previous version is preserved
     with history. Overwrite without trace is knowledge destruction.

  8. Never silently delete.
     When knowledge is no longer valid, mark it deprecated. Deletion
     removes the ability to understand why something was rejected.

  9. Scope every project-specific rule.
     Rules that apply only to one project MUST be scoped accordingly.
     Universal rules and project-specific rules live in different layers.

  10. Preserve why a decision was made.
      Every decision record explains the rationale. Future changes
      require understanding prior context. "Why" is as important as "what".

Principle Mapping:

  Anti-dump:     Principles 1, 2, 3, 5
  Anti-corruption: Principles 6, 7, 8
  Anti-amnesia:  Principles 9, 10

Rule Strength:

  MUST     — violation is critical (principles 6, 7, 8)
  SHOULD   — violation is warning (principles 1, 2, 3, 5)
  AVOID    — violation is minor issue (principles 4, 9, 10)

  AI review behavior:
    MUST violation    → block and report
    SHOULD violation  → warn and suggest fix
    AVOID violation   → note but allow

Navigation:
